# Rule stems the kit has shipped in the past and no longer ships.
#
# This list names candidates, not victims. sync-rules.sh will only remove
# a file here if BOTH of these hold:
#   - its bytes match a version the kit actually shipped under that name
#   - the operator passed --retire
#
# Being on this list is therefore not sufficient to delete anything. A
# project that independently wrote its own ponytail-playbook.mdc keeps it;
# so does anyone who locally edited the kit's copy. The kit destroyed real
# project rules twice by treating a filename as proof of ownership
# (process-kit-2d6), so ownership is now decided by content.
#
# Under --version, a rule newer than the pinned tag is treated the same
# way — a kit name whose bytes must still be proven — so a downgrade
# produces the rule set the tag describes without touching project files.
#
# One stem per line, no extension: the same entry retires both the
# Cursor .mdc and the Claude .md projection. Add an entry ONLY when the
# kit itself drops or renames a rule, and say what it became.
#
# Kit-internal: read from the kit clone by sync-rules.sh, never copied
# into a target repo. Maintainer obligation is declared in AGENTS.md.
#
# ponytail-playbook: renamed to graybeard-playbook during the kit rename
# wave. Verified as the only rule rename in kit history:
#   git log --diff-filter=R --name-status -- 'cursor/rules/*'
ponytail-playbook
